{"findings":["minidb/planner.py: `build_sources` reads `self.select.from_table`, but parser publishes the field as `Select.from_` (Select(distinct, items, from_, joins, where, group_by, having, order_by, limit, offset)). Every query dies with AttributeError before any QueryError can be raised -> use `self.select.from_`.","minidb/planner.py: `build_sources` checks `join.kind`, but the published Join node is `Join(table: TableRef, on: expr)` with no `kind` field; any query with a JOIN raises AttributeError -> delete the kind check (JOIN and INNER JOIN both parse to the same node) and read only `join.table` / `join.on`.","minidb/planner.py: `alias = ref.ref_name()` calls a method TableRef does not provide (published as the dataclass `TableRef(name: str, alias: str | None)`) -> use `alias = ref.alias if ref.alias is not None else ref.name`.","minidb/planner.py: AttributeError from the three items above escapes `plan()`, contradicting its own published promise that only QueryError leaves the planner; api.py's catch-all then reports every query as an internal error -> fix the field names and, defensively, wrap the body of `plan()` so non-QueryError exceptions are re-raised as QueryError.","minidb/planner.py: `IN` is parsed as `BinOp('in', x, Func('in_list', [items...]))`, and `validate` only special-cases a tuple right operand; the Func branch then looks 'IN_LIST' up in SCALAR_ARITY/AGGREGATE_ARITY and rejects it as an unknown function -> recognise Func name 'IN_LIST' in `validate` and validate its args as ordinary expressions instead of as a callable function.","minidb/planner.py: for a table with zero rows (`open_schema`) any column name binds successfully, so `SELECT nosuch FROM empty_t` yields NULL rather than the QueryError the spec requires for an unknown column; it also makes an unqualified name bind to the empty source when the real owner is another table -> bind against a real source first and raise `unknown column` when no non-empty source has the name.","minidb/engine.py: `_compare_keys` does `if result is None: continue`, i.e. an incomparable ORDER BY pair is silently treated as equal; the spec requires a mixed-type comparison in ORDER BY to be a QueryError -> raise QueryError('cannot order mixed types') when `compare(a, b)` returns None (and rely on functions.compare raising QueryError itself if that is its contract).","minidb/engine.py: `_from_clause` falls back to `condition = None`, producing a cross product when a join condition is missing; the planner guarantees `len(join_conditions) == len(sources) - 1` and INNER JOIN always has ON, so a missing condition is a bug being masked -> raise QueryError instead of joining unconditionally.","minidb/engine.py: `_eval`/`_attr`/`_function_name` dispatch on `type(node).__name__` and guess field names ('qualifier', 'table', 'prefix', 'alias', 'col', 'func', ...) rather than using the nodes.py fields the parser publishes (Column(table, name), Literal(value), Func(name, args)); this reaches around the published interface and will silently mis-evaluate if a node ever carries two of those attributes -> import the node classes and use isinstance plus the published field names.","minidb/engine.py: `_is_star` returns True for `node is None or node == '*'`, so a bare string literal '*' argument would be counted as COUNT(*); parser only emits Star(None) for COUNT(*) -> test `isinstance(node, Star)` only."],"target":"minidb/planner.py, minidb/engine.py (reviewed from minidb/api.py)"}